WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:07.500
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.310
I'm Maurie Samuels, the
Director of the Yale Program

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.360
for the Study of Anti-Semitism,

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.720
and on behalf of YPSA, the
Whitney Humanities Center,

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.680
and the department of Germanic
languages and literatures

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.900
at Yale, it's my great pleasure
to welcome you here today

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.810
to our Benjamin and Barbara
Zucker Lecture Series.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:31.670
Today's speaker Alex Ross,
a fellow Harvard '90 alum,

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:34.380
has written about music
for the New York Times,

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.730
the New Republic, the
London Review of Books,

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.680
among many other publications.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.700
And he's been the music critic
of the New Yorker Magazine

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.580
since the 1990s.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.400
Alex has won a slew of major prizes,

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.710
including the MacArthur Genius Grant.

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:50.533
His first book,

00:00:50.533 --> 00:00:52.570
"The Rest is Noise: Listening
to the 20th Century"

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:54.570
was published in 2007

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.410
and won the National Book
Critics Circle Award,

00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:59.030
was a finalist for the Pulitzer,

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:01.760
and was named one of the
10 best books of the year

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.250
by the New York Times.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:05.030
His second book, "Listen to This,"

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.510
a collection of his essays,

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:11.510
was published in 2010, and
his new book right here,

00:01:11.717 --> 00:01:14.360
"Wagnerism, Art and Politics
in the Shadow of Music"

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:19.270
from which his talk today is
drawn was published last year.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:22.350
This book took more than a decade of work,

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.940
but it's really amazing that
it didn't take even longer.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.780
Alex has seemingly tracked
down every reference

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.950
to Wagner in literature,
art, dance, philosophy,

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.580
you name it, over the past 150 years

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:38.080
and in every major European language.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.707
As he puts it on page 15, "The
book has socialist Wagner,

00:01:41.707 --> 00:01:45.530
"feminist Wagner, gay Wagner,
Black Wagner," et cetera.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:47.910
It's all there and analyzed

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:51.700
with a keen sensitivity to
the way that artistic forms

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.220
produce political and social meaning.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.760
The book is encyclopedic
but also incredibly engaging

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.410
and entertaining to read.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:04.410
It gives a grand overview of
modern and modernist culture

00:02:04.639 --> 00:02:06.360
through the lens of Wagner.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.080
So let me just say a word

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.670
about how things are gonna work today.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:13.570
Alex is gonna talk for
about 30 or 35 minutes,

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:16.360
and then I'll lead a discussion with him.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:21.360
Please submit questions
to me using the Q&amp;A button

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.580
at the bottom of your screen.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.440
And you can send questions at any time,

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:27.900
even during the talk.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.030
Please though, don't use the chat feature

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:31.530
or don't raise your hand.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:36.530
We can't see or hear you, and
we'll end promptly at six.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.720
So now I will turn it over to Alex.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.277
His title today is

00:02:40.277 --> 00:02:44.307
"The Unforgiven Wagner:
Jews and Antisemitism."

00:02:46.695 --> 00:02:50.200
- Thank you so much, Maurie,
for the very kind introduction,

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.840
and it is wonderful to be invited

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.558
to this distinguished series

00:02:56.558 --> 00:03:01.558
to talk about a very charged
and difficult question

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:07.020
which is the composer
Richard Wagner's relationship

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:11.340
with Jewishness, with anti-Semitism,

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.400
with the ideology of hate

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.230
that underpinned the Nazi regime,

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.520
with the Holocaust itself.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.520
Wagner is part of these stories
to a very disturbing extent,

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:32.210
but at the same time
he is a gigantic figure

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:37.210
in cultural history who has touched upon

00:03:39.202 --> 00:03:42.880
and has touched so many different worlds,

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.560
so many different people
of variegated backgrounds

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:52.450
in a way that it becomes, I think,

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:57.450
risky to categorize Wagner
purely as being aligned

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:07.560
with one ideological strain or another,

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.903
one political strain or another.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.560
And the mission that I undertook

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.062
with this book was to give as expansive

00:04:17.062 --> 00:04:19.970
and perhaps as contradictory a picture

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:22.187
of this phenomenon of Wagnerism,

00:04:23.164 --> 00:04:27.463
Wagner's influence outside
of music on the arts,

00:04:27.463 --> 00:04:29.970
on literature, on intellectual
life, on philosophy,

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:33.100
and of course on politics as well

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:38.100
to give as rich and ambiguous a picture.

00:04:40.038 --> 00:04:42.510
And really, I hope as faithful,

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:47.130
as representative a
picture of that influence

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:52.130
as I could to counter act to some degree,

00:04:52.758 --> 00:04:57.758
the more limiting perspective
that so many people have

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:00.770
on Wagner today, which
is to equate him entirely

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:05.051
with Nazi Germany, entirely
with anti-Semitism.

00:05:05.051 --> 00:05:08.776
That's really the one
thing that people now know

00:05:08.776 --> 00:05:13.033
about Wagner is that he was
Hitler's favorite composer.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.020
And while that is true

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:20.820
and while in no way do I
want to push that aspect

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.150
of the Wagner narrative to the side,

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:26.550
it becomes more and more
central as the book goes on.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:29.700
I do want to give the rest of the picture

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:34.700
and to under show how
extraordinarily widespread

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:40.280
Wagner's influence was at
the turn of the last century,

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:45.170
including his effect on
Jews, on African-Americans,

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:50.170
on gay people, on feminists,
on far left-wing figures,

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:54.020
socialists, communists anarchists,

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.490
sort of all across the political spectrum,

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:58.533
all across the cultural spectrum,

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:03.570
and then to ask how was
it that Wagner's image

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.500
became more and more confined

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:10.830
by the darker side of his legacy.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:13.570
And I want to make very
clear, I do not think

00:06:14.491 --> 00:06:18.076
this is a sort of unpleasant
accident that happened to him.

00:06:18.076 --> 00:06:20.490
It is something that
he brought on himself,

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.515
and the superb formulation of that

00:06:23.515 --> 00:06:26.615
I found in the writings of Thomas Mann,

00:06:26.615 --> 00:06:28.240
which really I think gets
to the heart of the problem

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:29.690
that we're facing with Wagner

00:06:30.892 --> 00:06:34.760
is that Wagner, Thomas Mann once said,

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:39.760
Wagner was a passionate and
devoted follower or admirer

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:46.460
of Wagner who was also at the
same time, deeply skeptical

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:51.460
of Wagner, had this enormous
ambivalence towards Wagner

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:55.770
in a way that I personally
find to be a model

00:06:56.950 --> 00:07:01.950
as someone who also listens
with great admiration

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:04.943
to Wagner's music and also
with a great deal of doubt.

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:11.940
Anyway, what Mann said was
that Wagner lent himself

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.273
to his own misuse.

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:17.600
And so I think that's quite
an elegant formulation

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:19.600
that on the one hand captures the extent

00:07:20.456 --> 00:07:23.590
to which Wagner was
manipulated, exploited distorted

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:28.090
in Nazi Germany especially,
but at the same time

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:29.920
he was involved in that process.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:33.800
And his anti-Semitism was
absolutely, of course,

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:38.800
at the core of the role that
he played in subsequent events,

00:07:44.112 --> 00:07:48.940
the means by which he became such a dark

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:52.420
and menacing figure in
so many people's lives.

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:55.750
Now there's a slew of
very complicated issues

00:07:56.610 --> 00:08:00.070
to be confronted here which I
have little hope of covering

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:03.990
in any detail in half an hour,

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:07.900
but I thought I could
give a very quick overview

00:08:09.391 --> 00:08:12.120
of some of the issues, sort
of lay out the territory

00:08:13.553 --> 00:08:16.730
and then really invite a
conversation on any points

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:20.820
that arrest people's attention

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:25.310
or raise questions or objections.

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:27.720
And just to continue this conversation

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:28.853
which is so important.

00:08:30.134 --> 00:08:32.192
And, you know, the thing that I would say

00:08:32.192 --> 00:08:37.192
about Wagner and the questions
that hang over Wagner

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:41.250
is that this is a very,
very old conversation.

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:43.560
It's been going on for a very long time.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:44.660
There's nothing new about it.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.890
I was actually a little bit amused.

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:50.820
In recent years especially as questions

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:54.236
were increasingly being
asked about famous artists,

00:08:54.236 --> 00:08:55.560
especially in the sort of popular world

00:08:55.560 --> 00:09:00.560
and the me too movement and
quote unquote cancel culture

00:09:00.796 --> 00:09:01.794
and everything else.

00:09:01.794 --> 00:09:02.813
And some people were asking me,

00:09:02.813 --> 00:09:04.990
are you worried about Wagner

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:07.440
as you've written this
big book about Wagner,

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:11.720
and, you know, now sort
of this kind of figure

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.956
is inviting more scrutiny.

00:09:13.956 --> 00:09:18.460
And of course, you know,
it's certainly not the case

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:22.140
that Wagner just up until
recent years has been beloved

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:23.620
and revered and then suddenly

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:25.460
people are asking questions about him.

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:29.290
No, the trouble with
Wagner began in the 1850s,

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:31.633
already in the 1850s, people are asking

00:09:31.633 --> 00:09:36.038
is Wagner too horrible for
us to listen to his music?

00:09:36.038 --> 00:09:38.530
Does it have anything to do with him?

00:09:38.530 --> 00:09:40.683
And yet, somehow he is still here.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:43.300
And so there's something about his work

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:46.163
that compels attention
and will not going away.

00:09:47.263 --> 00:09:48.610
I think precisely for that reason,

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:52.020
I think it's so valuable for us

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.140
to continue looking at Wagner

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:57.290
and even using this
endless debate over Wagner

00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:00.410
as a model for how we deal
with other troubling figures

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:01.733
in our cultural history.

00:10:03.045 --> 00:10:05.423
Because, you know,
especially in recent decades,

00:10:06.263 --> 00:10:09.770
a very sophisticated
methodology has emerged

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:12.420
for analyzing Wagner's anti-Semitism,

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:14.290
for analyzing the impact on his works.

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:17.443
And then for dealing with sort
of how we look at the works,

00:10:17.443 --> 00:10:19.343
how they are staged in the opera house

00:10:20.717 --> 00:10:24.902
and ways in which we can isolate

00:10:24.902 --> 00:10:26.226
and accentuate those questions

00:10:26.226 --> 00:10:27.680
and not just sort of brush them aside.

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:32.680
And so there's something in
this endless Wagner controversy,

00:10:34.986 --> 00:10:39.750
I think, that is edifying as we gaze

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:44.750
with a more skeptical eye at the heritage

00:10:46.663 --> 00:10:49.585
of Western civilization,

00:10:49.585 --> 00:10:52.180
which has sort of so many
troubling questions behind it

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:53.433
in so many ways.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.103
So to go back almost to the beginning,

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:02.990
the trouble started at 1850
when Wagner published an essay

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:07.760
in the distinguished magazine
"Neue Zeitschrift für Musik"

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.640
founded by Robert Schumann,

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:12.183
titled "Das Judenthum in der Musik"

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:16.290
Jewishness in Music.

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:17.840
This was not out of the blue.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:20.820
It was part of an ongoing debate
that had been taking place

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:24.450
in that publication and
other musical publications

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:29.323
about Jewish composers and
especially about Meyerbeer

00:11:30.978 --> 00:11:35.660
who had achieved and
still held enormous fame

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:38.083
as an opera composer.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:44.000
And there was a camp of
younger German composers

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:49.760
as well as music historians and critics

00:11:50.352 --> 00:11:55.290
who were trying to combat this influence

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:57.680
of Meyerbeer and establish space

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:02.673
for the radical new,
so-called new German school

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:07.300
of Wagner and Liszt.

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:09.970
And so Wagner contributed to this debate.

00:12:09.970 --> 00:12:12.713
Little of the vocabulary
of this essay was new.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:18.380
This idea, central to the
essay, that Jewish people

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:23.380
are somehow alien to
German musical culture,

00:12:24.117 --> 00:12:25.290
to sort of musical cultural,

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:27.593
European musical culture in general,

00:12:29.074 --> 00:12:31.500
that it's, somehow they
betray their otherness

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:37.020
as they attempt to master musical speech.

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:40.349
This was not a new argument.

00:12:40.349 --> 00:12:44.720
There was something new
about the sheer force,

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:47.210
the violence of Wagner's language,

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:49.200
and Wagner was a violent writer.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:50.290
He was insistent.

00:12:50.290 --> 00:12:55.223
He endlessly repeated his slogans and his,

00:12:57.467 --> 00:13:00.060
stichworte is the German
word, his buzzwords.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:04.440
He was very good at coining
these buzzwords and slogans.

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:06.800
And he would just sort of pound away.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:11.792
And so there's an insistence
of violence in this essay,

00:13:11.792 --> 00:13:14.370
Which contributed in some way, I think,

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:19.370
to the radicalization of
anti-Semitism in German discourse

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.970
in the middle of the 19th century.

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:24.080
The essay wasn't noticed.

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:27.485
It was talked about, and
actually within a few years,

00:13:27.485 --> 00:13:30.530
I should have mentioned that
it was published anonymously

00:13:30.530 --> 00:13:35.530
under a pseudonym, but Wagner's
authorship became known

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:39.090
and was actually discussed in England

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:43.408
by critics who were very
friendly to Mendelssohn's music

00:13:43.408 --> 00:13:45.940
in particular and who took great offense

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:49.913
at Wagner's critique of
Mendelssohn alongside Meyerbeer

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:54.773
as a Jewish composer, who
was someone not truly German

00:13:54.773 --> 00:13:56.850
or not sort of truly authentic.

00:13:56.850 --> 00:14:01.850
And so this is really the
beginning of the critique

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:07.270
of Wagner's anti-Semitism
as something quite dangerous

00:14:07.289 --> 00:14:09.313
in cultural discourse.

00:14:10.534 --> 00:14:13.140
But within a few years,
discussion ebbed away,

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:16.588
and it really wasn't very
widely discussed to begin with,

00:14:16.588 --> 00:14:20.760
this essay, and if Wagner
had left it at that,

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:23.230
I think we would be looking
at all of these issues

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:28.230
in a different way, but
he returned to the topic

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:33.190
of Jewishness and particularly
important was his decision

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:37.990
in 1869 to re-publish this
essay now under his own name,

00:14:37.990 --> 00:14:39.710
very widely distributed

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:43.390
in a way that called
enormous attention to it.

00:14:43.390 --> 00:14:47.169
And in the process, he added an afterward,

00:14:47.169 --> 00:14:52.169
which extended the attack on
Jews in quite nasty new ways.

00:14:54.913 --> 00:14:57.860
And his focus was now not
so much Jewish composers,

00:14:57.860 --> 00:15:02.400
but Jewish music critics,
especially the critic Hanslick

00:15:03.249 --> 00:15:07.163
who had emerged as his leading skeptic.

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:13.140
And from then on, Wagner
was known as an anti-Semite

00:15:15.590 --> 00:15:18.490
and has been known as such ever since.

00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:20.405
Yes, it was very controversial.

00:15:20.405 --> 00:15:23.813
There was a great deal of
distress over the essay,

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:30.763
including from among Wagner's allies.

00:15:31.873 --> 00:15:36.480
Just many people found it
quite dismaying and repulsive.

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:41.640
There was also amusement over this essay.

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:46.640
People found it ridiculous in many ways

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:51.670
to attack Mendelssohn who
really assumed such a major role

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:57.540
in sort of posthumously
this very grand status

00:15:57.960 --> 00:16:00.870
in the the musical canon.

00:16:00.870 --> 00:16:05.300
There were also sort of rumors swirling

00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:08.080
that Wagner himself might be Jewish,

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:11.770
and that this was all some
kind of psychological implosion

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:14.751
sort of taking place within him

00:16:14.751 --> 00:16:19.292
that he was becoming hysterical
on the subject of Jews,

00:16:19.292 --> 00:16:23.777
and I'll show on the screen-share here

00:16:26.032 --> 00:16:29.043
a somewhat disturbing cartoon.

00:16:34.446 --> 00:16:36.696
Such, and the title here is

00:16:39.123 --> 00:16:41.956
"Darwiniftifche Entwidlungslebre,"

00:16:43.037 --> 00:16:46.400
"Darwinian Evolutionary Theory."

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:50.910
And you can see pretty
easily what's happening here.

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:55.640
An anti-Semitic stereotype is evolving

00:16:57.180 --> 00:17:00.623
into the figure of Wagner.

00:17:01.805 --> 00:17:03.560
It is sort of an anti-Semitic image

00:17:04.667 --> 00:17:06.717
that also attacks Wagner's anti-Semitism.

00:17:08.243 --> 00:17:12.917
Rather a troubling, complex
phenomenon in itself.

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:19.530
And so there was this derision of Wagner

00:17:21.664 --> 00:17:26.060
that was seen, especially
in Vienna at the time.

00:17:27.020 --> 00:17:32.020
Antisemitism in 1869 had not achieved

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:37.630
the kind of lethal political strength

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:42.046
that it would achieve.

00:17:42.046 --> 00:17:43.653
And in a fairly short period of time,

00:17:44.722 --> 00:17:46.703
especially going into the 1880s.

00:17:47.723 --> 00:17:52.340
So there was a sense that
Wagner's version of anti-Semitism

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:57.340
was not quite aligned with
the political discourse

00:17:57.700 --> 00:18:01.330
at the end of the 1860s,
but things changed.

00:18:01.330 --> 00:18:06.330
After the crash of 1873, anti-Semitism

00:18:06.802 --> 00:18:09.310
really gathered strength

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:14.360
as a way of shifting blame onto Jews.

00:18:16.050 --> 00:18:20.980
Various other forces contributed
to this gathering intensity

00:18:20.980 --> 00:18:21.843
of anti-Semitism,

00:18:23.449 --> 00:18:28.140
and the shift from a more religiously

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:33.140
or economically based
form of anti-Jewishness.

00:18:35.686 --> 00:18:37.070
And I'm sure you've
discussed this kind of shift

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:39.750
often in the series

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:44.750
toward this pseudo-scientific,
biologically defined notion

00:18:48.846 --> 00:18:52.530
of Jewish otherness as a genetic otherness

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:55.540
which of course would become
the foundation of elimination,

00:18:55.540 --> 00:18:59.963
this anti-Semitism in the 20th century,

00:19:00.910 --> 00:19:04.967
this idea that there was
something ineradicably different

00:19:04.967 --> 00:19:09.686
about Jewish people that
no degree of assimilation

00:19:09.686 --> 00:19:13.590
or conversion would address.

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:16.490
Now a very big question in the discussion

00:19:16.490 --> 00:19:19.200
of Wagner's anti-Semitism is

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:23.010
did he himself complete this transition

00:19:23.966 --> 00:19:28.966
from the older form of
anti-Semitism to the newer one?

00:19:31.287 --> 00:19:32.471
And of course it has to be stressed

00:19:32.471 --> 00:19:33.540
that the older form of anti-Semitism

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:37.320
was by no means innocuous,

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:41.630
and thousands, millions of
people suffered and died

00:19:42.647 --> 00:19:47.170
under the force of that
version of anti-Semitism.

00:19:47.170 --> 00:19:49.390
But it is nonetheless an
important question, you know.

00:19:49.390 --> 00:19:53.980
Did Wagner himself undergo,
completed that evolution?

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:56.840
And my feeling is that he didn't quite.

00:19:56.840 --> 00:19:58.927
You can sort of have a sense reading

00:19:58.927 --> 00:20:01.290
"Das Judenthum in der Musik"

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:06.290
that he seems to be seeing
Jews as innately other,

00:20:08.926 --> 00:20:10.550
and no matter how hard they try,

00:20:10.550 --> 00:20:15.550
they cannot master the language
of German art, German music,

00:20:16.107 --> 00:20:18.441
and there's sort of something in them

00:20:18.441 --> 00:20:21.483
which cannot be overcome, and
that is a sort of troubling,

00:20:22.685 --> 00:20:25.840
early indicator of that kind
of biological mentality,

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:28.990
but then still in his later writings

00:20:28.990 --> 00:20:33.990
which are full of ghastly
language borrowed from Gobineau

00:20:35.890 --> 00:20:40.313
and other racial thinkers,
still there's this sense,

00:20:41.730 --> 00:20:42.720
the suggestion that he throws out

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:46.220
that somehow there could
be this transfiguration,

00:20:46.220 --> 00:20:48.270
this sort of transformation

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:52.617
that he says would raise
even the lowest races upwards

00:20:55.668 --> 00:21:00.220
under the blessed umbrella

00:21:00.220 --> 00:21:03.730
of the sort of spiritual emanation

00:21:03.730 --> 00:21:07.160
of a pure Christian idea,
the kind of emanation

00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:11.320
that one might find at
Bayreuth in the Festspielhaus

00:21:12.607 --> 00:21:15.220
at Bayreuth, and
performances of "Parsifal."

00:21:15.220 --> 00:21:20.220
And, you know, this is not
a very generous concession

00:21:21.400 --> 00:21:23.773
on Wagner's part by any means.

00:21:24.940 --> 00:21:27.530
But I think there is a sense that there's,

00:21:27.530 --> 00:21:29.490
that he doesn't quite go all the way

00:21:29.490 --> 00:21:31.140
in terms of this sort of movement

00:21:32.045 --> 00:21:34.203
toward the biological anti-Semitism.

00:21:34.203 --> 00:21:35.625
And of course, there's the fact

00:21:35.625 --> 00:21:37.630
that he had many Jews
around him in Bayreuth.

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:40.080
And this gets into another issue

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:44.407
of Wagner's own personal
relationships with Jews

00:21:44.407 --> 00:21:46.520
which are very complicated

00:21:47.628 --> 00:21:51.660
and sort of ultimately
hard to make sense of

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:53.163
in many ways.

00:21:54.310 --> 00:21:59.310
The, his Jewish friends and
especially subordinates,

00:21:59.370 --> 00:22:04.370
sort of helpers, underwent
a fair amount of humiliation

00:22:05.008 --> 00:22:06.593
in his presence.

00:22:07.748 --> 00:22:11.510
But at the same time, there
was a genuine friendship

00:22:11.510 --> 00:22:14.267
or sort of fondness on Wagner's part,

00:22:14.267 --> 00:22:17.993
and no one was compelling
him to, you know,

00:22:19.980 --> 00:22:22.590
undertake these relationships.

00:22:22.590 --> 00:22:24.250
There's something very
complicated going on

00:22:24.250 --> 00:22:26.830
sort of some of this sort of need

00:22:28.571 --> 00:22:29.831
to kind of have Jews around him,

00:22:29.831 --> 00:22:31.769
but also to control them

00:22:31.769 --> 00:22:34.573
or to sort of put them
in subordinate positions.

00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:41.640
Strange, dark psychological
questions with Wagner.

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:46.920
Jews were an obsession,
a lifelong obsession

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:48.370
that he could never let go of

00:22:50.210 --> 00:22:53.310
and that sort of at times
seemed to sort of dominate

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:56.113
his consciousness.

00:22:57.370 --> 00:22:59.511
And he believed that ultimately

00:22:59.511 --> 00:23:01.853
Jews were going to be victorious.

00:23:04.091 --> 00:23:06.870
And that sort of perhaps he felt

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:09.930
that when he himself had to submit

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:13.730
in some ways to Jewish presence
and to sort of control it

00:23:13.730 --> 00:23:16.960
as much as he could on
his own sort of territory.

00:23:16.960 --> 00:23:18.530
It's difficult to speculate,

00:23:18.530 --> 00:23:21.233
dangerous to speculate
exactly what was going on,

00:23:22.769 --> 00:23:25.254
but it was there's something
different about it.

00:23:25.254 --> 00:23:26.693
There's something singular about it

00:23:26.693 --> 00:23:30.987
as against so much of the
anti-Semitism that you find

00:23:32.740 --> 00:23:35.810
in Germany and Austria and
other European countries

00:23:37.872 --> 00:23:39.122
in this period.

00:23:40.266 --> 00:23:42.080
Another great question which arises,

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:47.063
a question for which I honestly
do not have a fixed answer

00:23:49.650 --> 00:23:54.200
is can one detect anti-Semitism
in the works themselves?

00:23:55.850 --> 00:24:00.112
And there's been a great deal of writing,

00:24:00.112 --> 00:24:03.430
a great deal of powerful
and intelligent scholarship

00:24:05.308 --> 00:24:08.313
on the question of characters
like Alberich and Mime,

00:24:11.270 --> 00:24:14.323
the dwarves, the conniving
dwarves and the ring,

00:24:16.272 --> 00:24:18.880
Beckmesser, the conniving Meistersinger,

00:24:21.759 --> 00:24:24.663
Merker, a musical judge,

00:24:25.669 --> 00:24:29.110
really sort of a metaphor
for a music critic

00:24:29.110 --> 00:24:30.997
in "Die Meistersinger,"

00:24:33.091 --> 00:24:37.670
perhaps Klingsor, the evil
sorcerer in "Parsifal,"

00:24:37.670 --> 00:24:39.943
certainly the figure of Kundry,

00:24:40.788 --> 00:24:45.788
the mysterious eternal
wanderer in "Parsifal."

00:24:45.833 --> 00:24:47.623
Are these Jewish figures?

00:24:48.700 --> 00:24:53.700
Very early on, there was open
speculation on this point.

00:24:53.731 --> 00:24:54.970
And actually there were protests

00:24:54.970 --> 00:24:56.120
at early performances of Meistersinger

00:24:56.120 --> 00:25:01.120
because Jewish opera-goers felt that,

00:25:01.508 --> 00:25:04.222
feared that Beckmesser was
going to turn out to be

00:25:04.222 --> 00:25:08.513
a Jewish stereotype.

00:25:09.790 --> 00:25:12.220
Somehow that that idea did not take hold

00:25:12.220 --> 00:25:16.764
even though it was sort
of in the public arena,

00:25:16.764 --> 00:25:18.110
and so after those early performances,

00:25:18.110 --> 00:25:21.610
it's very difficult to
find commentary, excuse me,

00:25:21.610 --> 00:25:24.630
commentary on Meistersinger
which brings up

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:27.985
this Jewish Beckmesser issue.

00:25:27.985 --> 00:25:30.168
So either in the sort of Jewish world

00:25:30.168 --> 00:25:33.565
or in the kind of antisemitic
press and commentary,

00:25:33.565 --> 00:25:34.780
even right into the Nazi period,

00:25:34.780 --> 00:25:36.030
you simply don't find it.

00:25:37.047 --> 00:25:39.570
The scholar David Dennis made
a very, very thorough search

00:25:39.570 --> 00:25:41.220
for such discussions of Beckmesser

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:43.490
and could not find them.

00:25:43.490 --> 00:25:44.630
In the case of Alberich and Mima,

00:25:44.630 --> 00:25:47.930
you do see mentions of the idea

00:25:47.930 --> 00:25:50.270
including from none
other than Gustav Mahler

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:54.900
that these are Jewish
caricatures of sorts,

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:56.950
but they were not terribly widespread.

00:25:56.950 --> 00:25:59.880
This was not kind of one
of the dominant lenses

00:25:59.880 --> 00:26:03.831
through which people
viewed the work of Wagner.

00:26:03.831 --> 00:26:07.471
It's really only in the
post-World War II era,

00:26:07.471 --> 00:26:10.510
the post-Holocaust era that
the question of Jewishness

00:26:10.510 --> 00:26:13.490
in the Wagner operas has come to the fore.

00:26:13.490 --> 00:26:17.410
The great philosopher Ted
Adorno was one of the first

00:26:18.348 --> 00:26:20.311
to advance this idea when he said

00:26:20.311 --> 00:26:25.010
all of the outcasts in
Wagner's work are Jews.

00:26:25.010 --> 00:26:27.390
And then as I mentioned,

00:26:27.390 --> 00:26:32.390
a number of significant scholars
have taken up this issue

00:26:32.687 --> 00:26:33.692
in recent decades.

00:26:33.692 --> 00:26:35.648
The question remains unsettled.

00:26:35.648 --> 00:26:37.648
It's a very powerful and plausible idea,

00:26:38.609 --> 00:26:43.609
but because there's no evidence
that Wagner intended it,

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:45.630
and Wagner was not the kind of person

00:26:46.742 --> 00:26:48.492
who kept his intentions to himself.

00:26:49.592 --> 00:26:50.425
We sort of have to assume

00:26:50.425 --> 00:26:53.500
that this was either a secretive

00:26:55.251 --> 00:26:58.070
or even a kind of unconscious
intention on his part,

00:26:58.070 --> 00:27:00.348
which may be so.

00:27:00.348 --> 00:27:01.260
I think there is something there,

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:04.080
but I find it very difficult to pin down

00:27:05.410 --> 00:27:06.760
and to be sort of
absolutely certain about.

00:27:06.760 --> 00:27:10.107
And I'm hesitant when
people sort of take it as

00:27:10.107 --> 00:27:12.060
sort of absolutely obvious and proven

00:27:12.060 --> 00:27:15.340
that the Wagner operas are anti-Semitic

00:27:15.340 --> 00:27:18.250
that they contain
anti-Semitic caricatures.

00:27:18.250 --> 00:27:20.403
I think that's going too far.

00:27:22.094 --> 00:27:27.094
And in terms of Wagner's
relationship with Nazism,

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:30.390
I don't think this is
something that I can go into

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:33.050
in any detail because this
is such a huge question

00:27:33.050 --> 00:27:36.750
in and of itself, but I
think it's worth noting

00:27:36.750 --> 00:27:41.560
that in terms of the particular rhetoric

00:27:42.440 --> 00:27:44.563
of Wagner's anti-Semitism,

00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:48.240
it was not one of the primary sources

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:50.720
on which the Nazis drew

00:27:52.431 --> 00:27:57.120
for their kind of anti-Semitic
sort of anthologies

00:27:57.120 --> 00:28:00.273
of kind of sort of anti-Semitic writings.

00:28:00.273 --> 00:28:01.553
Jewishness of music would be there.

00:28:02.734 --> 00:28:06.973
Goebbels would quote one of
the late essays on occasion.

00:28:08.091 --> 00:28:12.607
Wagner saying that the
Jews are the plastic demon

00:28:13.670 --> 00:28:16.280
of the downfall of mankind,

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:17.863
whatever that means exactly.

00:28:19.768 --> 00:28:22.190
Hitler never quoted Wagner
on the subject of Jews.

00:28:22.190 --> 00:28:24.790
He never made any mention
of Wagner's anti-Semitism.

00:28:25.750 --> 00:28:28.867
If he undoubtedly knew that
Wagner was anti-Semitic,

00:28:28.867 --> 00:28:30.963
he undoubtedly had read the essay,

00:28:30.963 --> 00:28:33.310
but he didn't find it
useful to quote Wagner

00:28:33.310 --> 00:28:34.968
on these points.

00:28:34.968 --> 00:28:37.668
And I think the reason for
that is sort of surprising,

00:28:41.092 --> 00:28:43.088
sort of sense that in Nazi Germany,

00:28:43.088 --> 00:28:48.088
Wagner is not a sort of Titan
of anti-Semitic thought.

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:55.530
His writing is strange,
confusing, contradictory,

00:28:55.530 --> 00:28:58.710
bewildering, and sort of lacking

00:28:58.710 --> 00:29:03.710
in the kind of cretinous
clarity that did you find

00:29:04.590 --> 00:29:08.170
in sort of anti-Semitic
writing of a later period.

00:29:08.170 --> 00:29:12.890
And it was more useful, Wagner
was more useful to the Nazis

00:29:12.890 --> 00:29:16.320
as this grand cultural figurehead

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:20.750
who seemed in some way or another
to anticipate Nazi Germany

00:29:20.750 --> 00:29:22.210
to sort of herald

00:29:23.050 --> 00:29:26.270
the sort of hyper
nationalist Nazi German State

00:29:27.795 --> 00:29:31.177
sort of rather than a sort
of more precise precursor,

00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:37.780
particularly with respect
to anti-Semitic ideology.

00:29:37.780 --> 00:29:41.355
There were some Nazis who disliked Wagner

00:29:41.355 --> 00:29:45.157
who found him sort of an
inauthentic anti-Semite.

00:29:45.157 --> 00:29:50.157
They found him decadent,
questionable on various grounds.

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:54.583
In previous periods there
were a number of anti-Semites,

00:29:55.595 --> 00:29:57.600
(Audio Blurs) and Paul de Lagarde

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:01.640
who cannot abide Wagner and
found him to be repulsive

00:30:02.998 --> 00:30:06.080
and sort of useless to their movement.

00:30:06.080 --> 00:30:11.080
And so by saying all this, I'm
not trying to separate Wagner

00:30:12.220 --> 00:30:15.760
from the history of anti-Semitism.

00:30:15.760 --> 00:30:16.593
He is there.

00:30:16.593 --> 00:30:18.640
He plays an important role,

00:30:18.640 --> 00:30:22.670
but I think it's a slightly
more marginal position

00:30:22.670 --> 00:30:27.670
than a kind of typical short
presentation of the matter

00:30:28.030 --> 00:30:29.040
would have you think

00:30:30.613 --> 00:30:34.090
that sort of Wagner was
Hitler's favorite composer.

00:30:34.090 --> 00:30:39.090
Wagner sort of dictated
anti-Semitism to Hitler

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:42.113
that he was an absolutely
formative presence.

00:30:43.453 --> 00:30:47.090
I think to sort of assign too
much responsibility to Wagner

00:30:47.090 --> 00:30:50.710
is to let everyone else
off the hook in a way.

00:30:50.710 --> 00:30:53.930
And, you know, the roots of anti-Semitism

00:30:53.930 --> 00:30:56.040
go very, very deep, back centuries

00:30:57.216 --> 00:30:58.934
not just in German culture and history

00:30:58.934 --> 00:30:59.767
but European culture and history,

00:31:00.613 --> 00:31:03.550
Christian history, and around the world.

00:31:03.550 --> 00:31:07.680
And so Wagner plays a role,
but not quite as large a role

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:09.963
as I think some would have you think.

00:31:11.050 --> 00:31:12.900
The last matter that I wanna touch on

00:31:14.137 --> 00:31:16.730
before taking up any of your questions is,

00:31:16.730 --> 00:31:18.190
in a way the most interesting one

00:31:18.190 --> 00:31:21.213
and the one that I spend
the most time on in the book

00:31:21.213 --> 00:31:23.355
which is Jewish Wagnerites.

00:31:23.355 --> 00:31:24.876
There were a great many of them,

00:31:24.876 --> 00:31:28.780
not just those who were
directly around Wagner

00:31:28.780 --> 00:31:32.290
in Bayreuth, and many
other places that he lived

00:31:33.137 --> 00:31:36.323
throughout his life, but in the audience,

00:31:37.897 --> 00:31:41.753
among some of the most vociferous,
enthusiastic Wagner fans.

00:31:44.912 --> 00:31:46.943
And in later generations,

00:31:48.660 --> 00:31:51.053
through especially into
the Fantasque period,

00:31:52.360 --> 00:31:54.873
a great many enthusiasts.

00:31:55.750 --> 00:32:00.621
Not necessarily worshipers,
but enthusiasts,

00:32:00.621 --> 00:32:01.810
people who took Wagner seriously

00:32:02.664 --> 00:32:05.820
and found him inspiring or
sort of thought-provoking

00:32:06.961 --> 00:32:07.857
in one way or another.

00:32:07.857 --> 00:32:10.490
One of them was Theodore Herzl
as I talk about in the book,

00:32:10.490 --> 00:32:12.710
and a sort of remarkable moment

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:16.575
in the history of Jewish Wagnerism
is when Herzl is in Paris

00:32:16.575 --> 00:32:19.290
in 1896, writing "Der Judenstaat,"

00:32:20.383 --> 00:32:25.383
the founding document
of the Zionist state,

00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:29.587
the prospectus of the Zionist
state, and at the same time

00:32:29.587 --> 00:32:33.050
"Tannhäuser," Wagner's
"Tannhäuser" is playing

00:32:33.050 --> 00:32:35.890
at the opera in Paris.

00:32:35.890 --> 00:32:39.630
And Herzl goes to see it
every time it's staged

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:40.990
throughout its run.

00:32:40.990 --> 00:32:45.330
And he says in his diary
that only on the nights

00:32:45.330 --> 00:32:50.330
when the opera was not performed
did he lack inspiration,

00:32:51.300 --> 00:32:53.810
lack conviction for what he was doing,

00:32:53.810 --> 00:32:55.010
for what he was writing.

00:32:55.880 --> 00:32:58.470
And so there's this sense of
which, by his own testimony,

00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:02.620
the music of "Tannhäuser" is emboldening,

00:33:02.620 --> 00:33:06.573
is empowering Herzl as he works.

00:33:07.777 --> 00:33:10.040
And this is something
that we find very often

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:11.390
at the turn of the century,

00:33:12.390 --> 00:33:15.623
sort of the late 19th century
into the early 20th century,

00:33:16.576 --> 00:33:18.850
especially younger people who go to Wagner

00:33:18.850 --> 00:33:23.453
and feel what W.E.B. Dubois,

00:33:24.342 --> 00:33:26.320
another Wagnerian enthusiast called,

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:29.330
a movement of power, a
sense of movement of power

00:33:29.330 --> 00:33:31.530
within the spectator,

00:33:31.530 --> 00:33:36.080
and a sense of visions
of future possibility,

00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:37.580
future greatness, sort of a feeling

00:33:37.580 --> 00:33:40.970
that one's ambitions are
mirrored back to oneself

00:33:40.970 --> 00:33:43.330
in more heightened form

00:33:43.330 --> 00:33:48.330
as one listens to Wagner and sees Wagner.

00:33:50.563 --> 00:33:54.980
And of course this mechanism
can take a much darker form.

00:33:54.980 --> 00:33:57.883
Adolf Hitler was exactly
one of those young people

00:33:57.883 --> 00:34:02.144
who felt vague feelings
of of future greatness

00:34:02.144 --> 00:34:04.694
when he first started seeing
Wagner operas in Linz.

00:34:05.859 --> 00:34:06.910
But nonetheless, it's just a
very wide spread phenomenon,

00:34:06.910 --> 00:34:08.790
and you find it with Jewish Wagnerites,

00:34:08.790 --> 00:34:11.040
and there isn't, it isn't necessarily

00:34:12.281 --> 00:34:15.957
a case of sort of the the
tortured or self-hating Jew.

00:34:17.170 --> 00:34:20.310
There's been a lot of discourse
about Jewish self-hatred

00:34:20.310 --> 00:34:25.310
and a lot of critiques of the
idea of Jewish self-hatred

00:34:25.600 --> 00:34:26.433
back and forth.

00:34:26.433 --> 00:34:30.930
Obviously, Wagner is a kind
of very promising territory

00:34:30.930 --> 00:34:33.040
in which to pursue that theme.

00:34:33.040 --> 00:34:38.040
And you do find a number of
Jews who are very conservative,

00:34:38.180 --> 00:34:42.159
who are seeking to sort of
assimilate themselves completely,

00:34:42.159 --> 00:34:43.880
who are disgusted by their Jewishness

00:34:43.880 --> 00:34:46.580
and want to somehow erase
it from their being,

00:34:46.580 --> 00:34:49.420
and listening to Wagner,
becoming absorbed in Wagner

00:34:49.420 --> 00:34:51.540
is part of that mechanism

00:34:51.540 --> 00:34:54.280
of sort of transforming themselves.

00:34:54.280 --> 00:34:56.030
And there's the very dark story

00:34:56.030 --> 00:35:00.590
of the philosopher Otto
Weininger who killed himself

00:35:00.590 --> 00:35:04.050
at a very young age after
writing what would become

00:35:05.158 --> 00:35:08.280
the best selling tracked
sex and character,

00:35:08.280 --> 00:35:13.280
racist and misogynist document
of startling strangeness.

00:35:17.221 --> 00:35:19.500
As we revisit it now, he was also gay.

00:35:19.500 --> 00:35:21.740
And then, so this,

00:35:21.740 --> 00:35:25.540
the phenomenon of the self-hating
Jewish Wagnerite is real

00:35:26.424 --> 00:35:29.380
and actually novelists and playwrights

00:35:29.380 --> 00:35:32.304
made fun of such a figure.

00:35:32.304 --> 00:35:35.920
You find it in Carl
Sternheim and Schnitzler

00:35:36.998 --> 00:35:38.150
and some others, but then you have others

00:35:38.150 --> 00:35:42.640
who were not in a sort of a
state of psychological agony

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:44.720
and torment as they listened to Wagner.

00:35:44.720 --> 00:35:48.415
Hetzel certainly seems to be one of those.

00:35:48.415 --> 00:35:50.162
And of course there are questions

00:35:50.162 --> 00:35:51.440
with Hetzel about his relationship

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:55.740
with Eastern European Jewishness,

00:35:55.740 --> 00:35:59.050
and his urge to sort of distance himself

00:35:59.920 --> 00:36:02.530
from sort of certain
forms of Jewish culture.

00:36:02.530 --> 00:36:06.404
Yes, but I don't think
anyone can realistically

00:36:06.404 --> 00:36:09.010
describe Hetzel as a self-hating Jew.

00:36:09.010 --> 00:36:13.630
And again, sort of Wagner was,

00:36:13.630 --> 00:36:17.250
he didn't seem to view Wagner
purely as an anti-Semite,

00:36:17.250 --> 00:36:20.614
even as he was very aware
of Wagner's anti-Semitism.

00:36:20.614 --> 00:36:22.769
It was a separation that
he could make in his mind,

00:36:22.769 --> 00:36:26.494
and you find that with a number
of other Jewish Wagnerites.

00:36:26.494 --> 00:36:28.944
And so this is something
where I think we need to

00:36:30.130 --> 00:36:35.130
look at the past especially
with a certain compassion

00:36:35.640 --> 00:36:37.560
and a certain sophistication,

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:42.560
and not with a kind of extreme
self-righteous judgmentalism.

00:36:44.455 --> 00:36:46.475
In the introduction of the book,

00:36:46.475 --> 00:36:50.214
I mention the late critic
Michael Andre Bernstein

00:36:50.214 --> 00:36:52.510
and his idea of back shadowing,

00:36:52.510 --> 00:36:56.310
this idea that we evaluate the literature

00:36:56.310 --> 00:37:00.570
of the pre-Holocaust era, and
he was talking specifically

00:37:00.570 --> 00:37:02.490
about Jewish literature
in this case, you know,

00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:05.900
through the lens of what came after,

00:37:05.900 --> 00:37:10.900
and judging sort of figures
of this period by, you know,

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:15.700
whether or not they were
sensitive to rising anti-Semitism

00:37:15.700 --> 00:37:17.740
or whether they were oblivious to it.

00:37:17.740 --> 00:37:19.610
And so I think in the same way

00:37:19.610 --> 00:37:24.030
when we look at, excuse
me, Jewish Wagnerism,

00:37:24.030 --> 00:37:26.180
we need to see its many varieties.

00:37:26.180 --> 00:37:30.413
And we need to realize that
no one or almost no one

00:37:30.413 --> 00:37:33.031
had any idea of what was coming,

00:37:33.031 --> 00:37:34.890
and anti-Semitism was simply not

00:37:35.750 --> 00:37:39.080
one of the really defining characteristics

00:37:39.080 --> 00:37:43.260
that the people would
name attached to Wagner

00:37:43.260 --> 00:37:44.383
in that period.

00:37:45.387 --> 00:37:47.463
It was something that a lot
of people were embarrassed by,

00:37:47.463 --> 00:37:50.140
just would sort of
prefer not to talk about.

00:37:50.140 --> 00:37:52.943
It was very important, and
it was very important in,

00:37:53.927 --> 00:37:58.270
there were radical anti-Semitic circles,

00:37:58.270 --> 00:38:02.910
but with this book, I'm
essentially presenting a vast

00:38:02.910 --> 00:38:05.450
and unresolved question of, you know,

00:38:05.450 --> 00:38:10.448
what do we do with this
titanic and troubling figure

00:38:10.448 --> 00:38:12.290
and his legacy and the sort of the

00:38:12.290 --> 00:38:14.620
all the contradictory
aspects of his legacy,

00:38:14.620 --> 00:38:18.720
and Wagner and Jews and anti-Semitism

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:22.837
is really the most obvious case study

00:38:25.430 --> 00:38:27.573
of Wagner's questionableness,

00:38:30.370 --> 00:38:31.787
sort of problematic nature.

00:38:31.787 --> 00:38:35.022
But there are many, it takes
many, many other forms.

00:38:35.022 --> 00:38:38.030
And I think there is a reason
why Wagner is still with us.

00:38:38.030 --> 00:38:40.862
And what I end up saying at
the end of the book is that

00:38:40.862 --> 00:38:45.730
a kind of demystification
takes place with Wagner.

00:38:45.730 --> 00:38:49.530
No one can really, no one
who's sensible and rational

00:38:49.530 --> 00:38:53.570
can any longer put Wagner
on some kind of pedestal

00:38:53.570 --> 00:38:55.540
and sort of worship him

00:38:56.624 --> 00:38:59.520
and sort of treat him as
a sort of a pure emanation

00:38:59.520 --> 00:39:01.683
of a higher sphere.

00:39:02.605 --> 00:39:03.708
We cannot forget the history.

00:39:03.708 --> 00:39:04.541
We cannot forget the context,

00:39:04.541 --> 00:39:05.850
but I think that's very healthy

00:39:07.069 --> 00:39:12.069
to always sort of treat
art as a historical

00:39:12.080 --> 00:39:15.350
and human phenomenon
and not to separate it,

00:39:15.350 --> 00:39:19.527
not to sort of elevate it and abstract it.

00:39:19.527 --> 00:39:21.630
And I think in our own
culture, in American culture,

00:39:21.630 --> 00:39:24.950
we have problems with our veneration

00:39:24.950 --> 00:39:27.250
of certain cultural figures,

00:39:27.250 --> 00:39:29.790
veneration of our own popular culture

00:39:29.790 --> 00:39:32.260
which we tend to think
of as pure and innocent

00:39:32.260 --> 00:39:36.340
and sort of coming up
from the sort of people,

00:39:36.340 --> 00:39:41.140
and sort of combating
the elite and aristocracy

00:39:43.270 --> 00:39:45.963
and sort of power and so on and so forth.

00:39:46.925 --> 00:39:48.720
But in fact, there are deep,
deep troubling questions

00:39:49.665 --> 00:39:53.117
about our own cultural
legacy and the sort of idea

00:39:55.290 --> 00:39:57.120
that I place at the end of the book

00:39:57.120 --> 00:40:02.120
is especially that we as
Americans have a great deal

00:40:02.420 --> 00:40:04.388
to learn from the case of Wagner,

00:40:04.388 --> 00:40:07.345
the famous case of Wagner
as we look at ourselves

00:40:07.345 --> 00:40:12.203
and as we look at our own
problematic cultural inheritance.

00:40:13.160 --> 00:40:15.780
So on that point I will come to an end

00:40:16.970 --> 00:40:21.970
and would be happy to field
any and all questions.

00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:23.649
It's Wagner.

00:40:23.649 --> 00:40:24.990
No one's gonna have any questions.

00:40:24.990 --> 00:40:27.190
- Exactly, no, there are a
lot of questions already,

00:40:27.190 --> 00:40:30.650
so I will try to synthesize them.

00:40:30.650 --> 00:40:32.900
And that was fantastic.

00:40:32.900 --> 00:40:35.460
So thank you so much for that.

00:40:35.460 --> 00:40:39.302
I think you really gave
us a lot to think about

00:40:39.302 --> 00:40:40.860
and a great way to kind of, you know,

00:40:40.860 --> 00:40:45.020
think about the question,
the problem of Wagner.

00:40:45.020 --> 00:40:48.964
So to start out, I think,
can you say a little bit more

00:40:48.964 --> 00:40:51.824
about the context for the essay,

00:40:51.824 --> 00:40:56.366
for the 1850 "Das Judenthum
in der Musik" essay?

00:40:56.366 --> 00:40:58.970
So why exactly did he
have it in for Meyerbeer?

00:40:58.970 --> 00:41:02.010
I mean, Meyerbeer was nice
to him in a lot of ways.

00:41:02.010 --> 00:41:05.600
Like where was all this
hatred coming from?

00:41:05.600 --> 00:41:07.750
- Yeah, well, I sort of
skipped over the question

00:41:07.750 --> 00:41:12.750
of the origins of Wagner's
anti-Semitism before 1850,

00:41:14.070 --> 00:41:18.270
and it was sort of creeping
to the forefront, I think,

00:41:18.270 --> 00:41:21.083
in the 1840s.

00:41:21.083 --> 00:41:23.185
You know, there was also, there
was a lot of anti-Semitism

00:41:23.185 --> 00:41:25.050
on the left, and so Wagner
was a part of the left

00:41:25.050 --> 00:41:29.564
in the 1840s and participated
in the revolutions of 1848

00:41:29.564 --> 00:41:32.640
and had to leave Germany in 1849

00:41:32.640 --> 00:41:34.573
and spent more than a decade in exile.

00:41:35.410 --> 00:41:40.410
And as he was aligned with
sort of democratic ideas

00:41:43.284 --> 00:41:44.683
and sort of revolutionary ideas,

00:41:44.683 --> 00:41:47.610
at the same time he was
picking up anti-Semitism

00:41:47.610 --> 00:41:49.524
from figures on the left.

00:41:49.524 --> 00:41:53.830
And of course we have the
issue of Karl Marx's own essay

00:41:53.830 --> 00:41:57.040
on the Jewish question,
and the far less radical

00:41:58.280 --> 00:42:00.820
kind of anti-Jewish language
that you find there.

00:42:00.820 --> 00:42:02.083
So it was circulating.

00:42:03.238 --> 00:42:04.133
And it was like that may have been

00:42:05.739 --> 00:42:07.160
sort of some of the
context in which Wagner

00:42:07.160 --> 00:42:07.993
was first picking up this discourse,

00:42:07.993 --> 00:42:09.290
but then it was fused

00:42:09.290 --> 00:42:12.440
with his personal
obsessions and resentments,

00:42:12.440 --> 00:42:15.630
and so this is where Meyerbeer
comes into the picture.

00:42:15.630 --> 00:42:17.663
He left Germany in 1849.

00:42:18.540 --> 00:42:22.280
Shortly after he saw Meyerbeer,

00:42:22.280 --> 00:42:26.360
Meyerbeer's "Le Prophète" in Paris,

00:42:26.360 --> 00:42:30.250
and it was a huge success,
and he was enraged.

00:42:30.250 --> 00:42:31.820
And I think the sort of contrast

00:42:31.820 --> 00:42:35.860
between Meyerbeer's ongoing
success as a German composer

00:42:35.860 --> 00:42:40.307
who had settled in Paris
and his own failure

00:42:41.440 --> 00:42:45.540
and sort of outcast, outsider status

00:42:45.540 --> 00:42:50.540
set off some kind of rage in him, I think.

00:42:50.778 --> 00:42:54.377
So I failed to say that
this sort of anti-Semitism

00:42:54.377 --> 00:42:56.159
was already becoming
part of his discourse,

00:42:56.159 --> 00:42:59.550
but sort of it was that
point, that kind of jealousy

00:42:59.550 --> 00:43:02.870
it sort of, at which
it became radicalized,

00:43:02.870 --> 00:43:05.479
but it wasn't just about Meyerbeer.

00:43:05.479 --> 00:43:08.030
He just fell into a psychological
habit of blaming Jews,

00:43:08.030 --> 00:43:09.960
scapegoating Jews for his own setbacks

00:43:09.960 --> 00:43:14.150
which were so often created
by his own profligacy

00:43:14.150 --> 00:43:18.810
and combativeness and sort
of general sort of messiness

00:43:18.810 --> 00:43:20.970
in terms of how he dealt with the world.

00:43:20.970 --> 00:43:25.820
He would find a way to blame
Jews for those setbacks.

00:43:25.820 --> 00:43:30.330
And this became a recurring
kind of psychological obsession.

00:43:30.330 --> 00:43:31.590
So it was kind of a mix

00:43:31.590 --> 00:43:34.610
of these sort of environmental forces

00:43:34.610 --> 00:43:39.470
kind of working around him and
his own sort of compulsion,

00:43:42.001 --> 00:43:43.720
personal compulsion coming to the fore.

00:43:43.720 --> 00:43:46.313
And it's that compulsion,
which sort of gives,

00:43:48.894 --> 00:43:52.101
which gives that essay and
sort of all his subsequent

00:43:52.101 --> 00:43:53.120
sort of writings on Jews,

00:43:53.120 --> 00:43:55.190
it's what makes them so disturbing

00:43:55.190 --> 00:43:58.260
because it's not a theory.

00:43:58.260 --> 00:44:00.230
It's not an ideological ideology.

00:44:00.230 --> 00:44:02.450
It's a passion.

00:44:02.450 --> 00:44:05.320
This is something that comes
from very deep within him,

00:44:05.320 --> 00:44:08.118
and he confessed that in a
letter to Liszt, you know.

00:44:08.118 --> 00:44:09.418
It's vital to him.

00:44:09.418 --> 00:44:10.770
He needs, he needs this hatred.

00:44:10.770 --> 00:44:15.357
It fuels him, and you find
this in other artistic figures

00:44:15.357 --> 00:44:18.457
with when anti-Semitism comes to the fore.

00:44:18.457 --> 00:44:19.430
You find it in T.S. Eliot.

00:44:19.430 --> 00:44:21.580
It's very disturbing to see anti-Semitism

00:44:21.580 --> 00:44:23.480
becoming part of the creative process.

00:44:26.160 --> 00:44:31.160
- Yeah, I mean, you know,
you also sort of, you know,

00:44:31.180 --> 00:44:32.840
it seems to me like, you know,

00:44:32.840 --> 00:44:34.570
there's this phenomenon here

00:44:35.531 --> 00:44:36.480
of a kind of relation to capitalism.

00:44:36.480 --> 00:44:38.080
The Jews are kind of representing

00:44:38.080 --> 00:44:39.960
this sort of relation to capitalism

00:44:39.960 --> 00:44:44.290
that's inspiring a lot of intense
emotions, not just in him,

00:44:44.290 --> 00:44:47.217
but you know, in other people too,

00:44:47.217 --> 00:44:49.399
including a certain
kind of ambivalence too.

00:44:49.399 --> 00:44:50.290
So they're kind of desiring

00:44:50.290 --> 00:44:52.450
this sort of new
relationship to the market

00:44:52.450 --> 00:44:55.096
at the same time that
they're sort of hating that

00:44:55.096 --> 00:44:56.097
in themselves.

00:44:56.097 --> 00:44:59.541
So a few questions about the essay itself.

00:44:59.541 --> 00:45:02.530
So someone asked whether isn't it true

00:45:02.530 --> 00:45:06.280
that he gives some praise for,

00:45:06.280 --> 00:45:07.670
I'll maybe ask these
three questions together,

00:45:07.670 --> 00:45:11.120
and you can, you know,
answer them if you want.

00:45:11.120 --> 00:45:13.240
So isn't it true that he gives some praise

00:45:13.240 --> 00:45:17.240
for Jewish virtuosic instrumentalists

00:45:17.240 --> 00:45:20.760
at the same time as he, you know,

00:45:20.760 --> 00:45:25.217
heaps invective on
composers like Meyerbeer?

00:45:25.217 --> 00:45:27.877
And although he doesn't
name them, I don't think.

00:45:27.877 --> 00:45:30.076
My memory is he doesn't
name Meyerbeer, but--

00:45:30.076 --> 00:45:34.410
- He doesn't, yeah, which is
sort of all the more cowardly

00:45:34.410 --> 00:45:36.060
that he wrote this anonymous essay,

00:45:36.060 --> 00:45:38.147
attacking Meyerbeer
without naming Meyerbeer.

00:45:38.147 --> 00:45:41.190
But everyone knew exactly
who he was talking about.

00:45:41.190 --> 00:45:43.258
- And then, so isn't it true

00:45:43.258 --> 00:45:45.550
that he has some admiration
for Jewish performers?

00:45:45.550 --> 00:45:49.539
- Mildly, I mean, virtuosity
is a charged word.

00:45:49.539 --> 00:45:52.512
You know, it sort of can
become sort of mere virtuosity,

00:45:52.512 --> 00:45:53.910
kind of mere mechanical brilliance

00:45:53.910 --> 00:45:56.657
which is soulless, you know,

00:45:56.657 --> 00:45:58.461
and he said the same
thing about Italian opera.

00:45:58.461 --> 00:46:02.177
There was this sort of brilliant
display that's meaningless,

00:46:02.177 --> 00:46:06.614
and, you know, covers up
kind of the, you know,

00:46:06.614 --> 00:46:11.150
cultural vacancy and is not
kind of the true, hearty voice

00:46:11.150 --> 00:46:13.223
of the folk.

00:46:14.250 --> 00:46:18.850
It's sort of this sort
of aristocratic frippery.

00:46:18.850 --> 00:46:23.156
And so, yeah, he has sort
of mild, mild praise,

00:46:23.156 --> 00:46:25.473
but I wouldn't put sort of
too much emphasis on that.

00:46:25.473 --> 00:46:27.480
You know, he also, he
also praises to a degree

00:46:28.740 --> 00:46:33.740
Birna and Heine, really
for being so scathing

00:46:33.770 --> 00:46:37.050
toward themselves, sort of becoming models

00:46:37.050 --> 00:46:41.180
of Jewish self-hatred sort of almost.

00:46:41.180 --> 00:46:43.090
And so that's another, you know, and he,

00:46:43.090 --> 00:46:45.410
he did, I mean, he could
never bring himself

00:46:45.410 --> 00:46:48.751
to fully praise Heine, but
he took an enormous amount

00:46:48.751 --> 00:46:49.830
from Heine and sort of elements,

00:46:49.830 --> 00:46:53.003
so many elements of the early
operas, "Flying Dutchman"

00:46:53.916 --> 00:46:56.856
and "Tannhäuser" in
particular come from Heine.

00:46:56.856 --> 00:47:00.080
And I think there was, I think the essay

00:47:00.080 --> 00:47:03.762
is trying to sound like Heine in some way.

00:47:03.762 --> 00:47:05.980
It is sort of attempting
to sort of model itself

00:47:05.980 --> 00:47:08.880
on this brilliant polemic

00:47:10.199 --> 00:47:13.061
that Heine and Birna both practiced.

00:47:13.061 --> 00:47:16.293
And that he just falls short
because it's just not funny.

00:47:17.781 --> 00:47:18.614
I mean, it's not just not funny

00:47:18.614 --> 00:47:20.543
because of everything that came after,

00:47:20.543 --> 00:47:24.163
it's just not good comic
writing by any standard.

00:47:25.420 --> 00:47:26.373
And it's just, it's villainous,

00:47:27.441 --> 00:47:29.663
and it's kind of plodding and ponderous,

00:47:30.910 --> 00:47:34.123
and so it's, but I think that
there was some emulation,

00:47:35.380 --> 00:47:36.998
hidden emulation there.

00:47:36.998 --> 00:47:38.640
- So, you know, in the essay, he says,

00:47:38.640 --> 00:47:43.361
not only the Jews are
alien to German culture,

00:47:43.361 --> 00:47:45.970
but he also implies that they're incapable

00:47:45.970 --> 00:47:48.460
of producing real art, right?

00:47:48.460 --> 00:47:52.793
That they can only ever
imitate or kind of parody,

00:47:53.780 --> 00:47:56.960
and, you know, so there's
a question about whether,

00:47:56.960 --> 00:47:59.230
you know, that's definitely
a trope that you see

00:47:59.230 --> 00:48:01.699
in later anti-Semites.

00:48:01.699 --> 00:48:04.074
So like, you know, I'm thinking
of someone like Drehmel,

00:48:04.074 --> 00:48:06.824
you know, at the end of the
19th century and certainly,

00:48:08.021 --> 00:48:09.704
you know, into the 20th.

00:48:09.704 --> 00:48:11.140
So, but is that, do you know
whether that's new with Wagner?

00:48:11.140 --> 00:48:11.973
Is he?

00:48:13.025 --> 00:48:14.623
- That's not new, I mean, Franz Brendel.

00:48:16.084 --> 00:48:17.730
the editor of the "Neue
Zeitschrift für Musik"

00:48:17.730 --> 00:48:20.900
had said the same thing
in an earlier essay.

00:48:20.900 --> 00:48:23.470
I think also Theodor Uhlig, another,

00:48:23.470 --> 00:48:26.580
especially Theodor Uhlig
rather than Brendel

00:48:26.580 --> 00:48:31.470
had said, really made the same
claim about Jewish musicians,

00:48:31.470 --> 00:48:34.280
Jewish composers in an earlier essay

00:48:34.280 --> 00:48:35.963
that had set off this debate.

00:48:36.901 --> 00:48:38.782
And there were, there was another critic

00:48:38.782 --> 00:48:42.980
Ludvig Bishop who was defending
Mendelssohn and Meyerbeer

00:48:42.980 --> 00:48:47.980
and saying this
anti-Jewishness is ridiculous.

00:48:48.170 --> 00:48:52.464
And sort of hitting back in that degree,

00:48:52.464 --> 00:48:54.870
and so that's where Wagner stepped in,

00:48:54.870 --> 00:48:57.683
but no, this point had already been made

00:48:57.683 --> 00:48:58.730
specifically about Jewish composers,

00:48:58.730 --> 00:49:01.210
but Wagner fleshed it out certainly.

00:49:01.210 --> 00:49:06.210
He went on at greater length,
and he, that vehemence,

00:49:07.380 --> 00:49:11.905
that repetitiveness kind
of solidified this idea.

00:49:11.905 --> 00:49:12.980
It was no longer something

00:49:12.980 --> 00:49:14.450
that had been thrown out in passing

00:49:14.450 --> 00:49:17.622
in the middle of this
back and forth debate,

00:49:17.622 --> 00:49:22.145
in-fighting among kind
of German music critics

00:49:22.145 --> 00:49:22.990
and commentators.

00:49:22.990 --> 00:49:26.360
It became, it's, the
essay was just long enough

00:49:26.360 --> 00:49:29.030
that it just became a
statement on its own.

00:49:29.030 --> 00:49:32.530
And people forgot about
the context of the various

00:49:32.530 --> 00:49:34.800
sort of polemics back and forth,

00:49:34.800 --> 00:49:36.619
especially when it was being published.

00:49:36.619 --> 00:49:37.452
Then all of that,

00:49:37.452 --> 00:49:39.450
all of that prior
discussion had disappeared.

00:49:39.450 --> 00:49:42.243
And so, although I wouldn't say that it's,

00:49:43.236 --> 00:49:45.963
that it was anything
particularly original about the,

00:49:46.837 --> 00:49:49.970
just purely the content of the
claims that Wagner is making,

00:49:49.970 --> 00:49:54.970
in terms of this, the sweeping
cultural attack on Jews

00:49:56.130 --> 00:49:59.700
as infiltrators, as
imposters in German culture,

00:49:59.700 --> 00:50:02.763
the scale on which he
carried it out, that was new.

00:50:04.519 --> 00:50:05.630
- Yeah, and he, you know, quickly,

00:50:05.630 --> 00:50:09.018
there was a question about
like how far he went.

00:50:09.018 --> 00:50:11.250
You know, did he call for extermination?

00:50:11.250 --> 00:50:14.881
I know Cosima is not, you know,

00:50:14.881 --> 00:50:16.960
she even goes, you know,
she goes pretty far too.

00:50:16.960 --> 00:50:19.513
But how far does his anti-Semitism go?

00:50:21.098 --> 00:50:22.717
- I think there have been claims

00:50:22.717 --> 00:50:24.717
that especially the ending of this essay

00:50:25.777 --> 00:50:27.987
where he calls for "der untergang,"

00:50:29.010 --> 00:50:32.727
the untergang is a
complicated German word.

00:50:32.727 --> 00:50:35.130
"Going under" is the literal translation,

00:50:35.130 --> 00:50:39.260
but it also means kind
of downfall, destruction.

00:50:39.260 --> 00:50:41.260
It can also be sort of
a philosophical idea

00:50:41.260 --> 00:50:44.600
of sort of this kind of downfall

00:50:44.600 --> 00:50:46.720
that turns into this transformation.

00:50:46.720 --> 00:50:49.530
Hegel and sort of others sort
of use the concept this way.

00:50:49.530 --> 00:50:51.990
It's sort of hard to read, you know,

00:50:51.990 --> 00:50:55.923
and so it can be read in
several different ways.

00:50:56.780 --> 00:50:59.660
I think in the context,
it seems as though Wagner

00:50:59.660 --> 00:51:01.474
is in fact talking about

00:51:01.474 --> 00:51:05.100
not quite sort of assimilation,

00:51:05.100 --> 00:51:07.220
but this idea that he returns to,

00:51:07.220 --> 00:51:10.359
is sort of at the at the
end in his later essay,

00:51:10.359 --> 00:51:11.619
some kind of,

00:51:11.619 --> 00:51:14.780
some kind of apocalyptic
spiritual transformation

00:51:14.780 --> 00:51:18.997
which would somehow
just solve this problem.

00:51:18.997 --> 00:51:20.563
And it can seem menacing.

00:51:21.621 --> 00:51:23.190
I think to equate it with the Holocaust

00:51:23.190 --> 00:51:27.580
or to equate it with a
sort of genocidal program

00:51:27.580 --> 00:51:32.580
is just, makes no sense
whatsoever in this context.

00:51:33.220 --> 00:51:34.599
And no one treated it as such.

00:51:34.599 --> 00:51:36.010
You don't find Nazi theorists

00:51:36.010 --> 00:51:39.942
that are citing the ending of this essay,

00:51:39.942 --> 00:51:43.360
but it's chilling, you know,
just because of the way it ends

00:51:43.360 --> 00:51:44.650
with this word

00:51:44.650 --> 00:51:48.433
which sort of clangs out on the page.

00:51:49.950 --> 00:51:53.420
It's, the vehemence of it is disturbing.

00:51:53.420 --> 00:51:55.130
Later, you know, it emerged,

00:51:55.130 --> 00:51:57.850
and when Cosima's diaries were published,

00:51:57.850 --> 00:51:58.743
that he had made,

00:51:59.980 --> 00:52:04.980
he had joked about the
terrible theater fire in Vienna

00:52:06.490 --> 00:52:10.900
where hundreds of people
died including many Jews,

00:52:10.900 --> 00:52:14.270
and he'd sort of joked
about it and said something

00:52:14.270 --> 00:52:18.550
to the effect of all Jews should be burned

00:52:18.550 --> 00:52:20.697
at a performance of "Nathan the Wise."

00:52:21.900 --> 00:52:25.018
You know, an utterly
horrible thing to say,

00:52:25.018 --> 00:52:27.930
but again to sort of go from there

00:52:27.930 --> 00:52:31.200
to sort of he is plotting
the Holocaust, I think,

00:52:31.200 --> 00:52:33.550
is to go too far.

00:52:33.550 --> 00:52:36.133
And, so there were, he
had violence, I think,

00:52:37.049 --> 00:52:39.671
violent fantasies and
sort of violent thoughts,

00:52:39.671 --> 00:52:43.780
but it's just, in no way,
I think, can you really

00:52:44.712 --> 00:52:49.712
collect these sort of scattered
utterances into a program.

00:52:50.073 --> 00:52:52.691
Of course this was a remark
that emerged in the 1970s

00:52:52.691 --> 00:52:54.171
and sort of had nothing to do

00:52:54.171 --> 00:52:55.821
with the sort of earlier history.

00:52:57.009 --> 00:52:58.820
- So I was really fascinated
by your discussion

00:52:58.820 --> 00:53:02.149
of Afro Wagnerism, you know.

00:53:02.149 --> 00:53:05.210
And the fascination of
prominent Black intellectuals

00:53:05.210 --> 00:53:09.443
for Wagner especially W.E.B.
Dubois, who you, you know,

00:53:10.711 --> 00:53:13.000
describe going to Bayreuth in 1936.

00:53:13.000 --> 00:53:17.010
And, you know, so can you say
a little bit more about that

00:53:18.043 --> 00:53:20.040
and about, and did Dubois,

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:22.940
like was he aware of the
Wagner's anti-Semitism?

00:53:22.940 --> 00:53:24.861
Did he comment on it?

00:53:24.861 --> 00:53:26.298
Like how did, how did?

00:53:26.298 --> 00:53:29.400
- Yeah, it's a fascinating case.

00:53:29.400 --> 00:53:32.625
And this sort of emerged as
I was working on this chapter

00:53:32.625 --> 00:53:35.341
of the book where I talked
about Wagner race and racism,

00:53:35.341 --> 00:53:36.174
the sixth chapter of the book,

00:53:36.174 --> 00:53:38.310
it was originally really
gonna be all about

00:53:39.197 --> 00:53:40.030
the Jewish question.

00:53:41.106 --> 00:53:46.106
And I thought I'd mentioned
Black Wagnerites as well

00:53:46.299 --> 00:53:50.632
because I was aware of Dubois
and his love of Wagner.

00:53:50.632 --> 00:53:52.810
But then it sort of took
on greater weight for me,

00:53:52.810 --> 00:53:57.590
as I explored more of Dubois
and his commentary on Wagner,

00:53:57.590 --> 00:53:59.170
and as I found other figures,

00:53:59.170 --> 00:54:02.469
other Black figures,
intellectuals and artists.

00:54:02.469 --> 00:54:04.750
Who had a relationship with Wagner,

00:54:04.750 --> 00:54:05.843
Alain Locke,

00:54:12.710 --> 00:54:15.363
Shirley Graham who later married Dubois,

00:54:16.800 --> 00:54:19.643
Langston Hughes was very fond of Tristan.

00:54:22.589 --> 00:54:26.786
Ralph Ellison in his
youth listened to Wagner.

00:54:26.786 --> 00:54:29.036
And so it was something
of a motif, you know,

00:54:29.892 --> 00:54:33.650
among the Black intellectuals
of this certain generation.

00:54:34.500 --> 00:54:38.285
And so I was curious about, so
what do they find in Wagner?

00:54:38.285 --> 00:54:40.486
And then I discovered
this extraordinary figure

00:54:40.486 --> 00:54:43.144
of Luranah Aldridge, the Contralto

00:54:43.144 --> 00:54:46.447
who was contracted to
sing at Bayreuth in 1896.

00:54:46.447 --> 00:54:49.727
she was the daughter of the
great Black Shakespearian actor

00:54:49.727 --> 00:54:51.289
Ira Aldridge.

00:54:51.289 --> 00:54:53.642
And despite the fact that
Cosima was, if anything,

00:54:53.642 --> 00:54:56.273
more racist than her husband,

00:54:57.270 --> 00:54:59.060
she was gonna sing one of the Valkyries

00:54:59.060 --> 00:55:02.507
in the Ring at Bayreuth in
1896 until she fell sick.

00:55:02.507 --> 00:55:05.082
And I was able to reconstruct
her story in a way

00:55:05.082 --> 00:55:06.985
that hadn't been done before.

00:55:06.985 --> 00:55:09.130
And so I began to feel that
this was actually a bigger theme

00:55:09.130 --> 00:55:11.565
and should have a bigger
presence in this chapter,

00:55:11.565 --> 00:55:12.690
but of course Dubois
is at the heart of it.

00:55:12.690 --> 00:55:15.087
And really what's going on with Dubois

00:55:15.087 --> 00:55:16.550
is this sort of mechanism
that I discussed earlier

00:55:16.550 --> 00:55:21.550
of a figure from another
background, not a white German,

00:55:25.350 --> 00:55:30.350
nonetheless finding inspiration
in Wagner's mythic apparatus

00:55:32.887 --> 00:55:37.887
and seeing how this brilliant
modernization of myth

00:55:40.230 --> 00:55:42.640
that takes place in Wagner's operas,

00:55:42.640 --> 00:55:45.809
especially The Ring could be emulated.

00:55:45.809 --> 00:55:47.086
And so it's about emulation.

00:55:47.086 --> 00:55:48.468
And so what he talks about with Wagner

00:55:48.468 --> 00:55:51.564
is he sees a sort of
future great Black art

00:55:51.564 --> 00:55:54.006
that sort of uses the mythic past

00:55:54.006 --> 00:55:57.250
especially sort of, you know,
African myths and legends

00:55:57.250 --> 00:56:01.630
as Wagner used Nordic
and Germanic legends.

00:56:04.310 --> 00:56:07.190
I wonder whether Dubois
might've been impressed

00:56:07.190 --> 00:56:12.143
by the film "Black Panther" in this regard

00:56:13.185 --> 00:56:15.664
because sort of this is
something of what's happening

00:56:15.664 --> 00:56:20.664
in that case, but in
any event, it was that,

00:56:20.889 --> 00:56:24.840
it was, I think there's also
kind of slightly kind of

00:56:24.840 --> 00:56:28.120
insolent or defiant elements to Dubois,

00:56:28.120 --> 00:56:31.901
sort of stepping in and
saying this is mine.

00:56:31.901 --> 00:56:33.770
You know, this, these masterpieces

00:56:33.770 --> 00:56:37.150
of white Western civilization
also belong to me.

00:56:37.150 --> 00:56:37.983
I can take them.

00:56:37.983 --> 00:56:40.120
I can make use of them,
convert them to my own ends.

00:56:40.120 --> 00:56:42.501
So I think that's something also

00:56:42.501 --> 00:56:44.023
what's going on with Herzl, you know.

00:56:44.023 --> 00:56:49.023
And so there's a certain
defiance and pride

00:56:49.185 --> 00:56:52.500
in how he takes hold of Wagner.

00:56:52.500 --> 00:56:55.360
And then this extraordinary
story that appears

00:56:55.360 --> 00:56:58.547
in "The Souls of Black Folk,"
"Of the Coming of John,"

00:56:59.563 --> 00:57:02.400
very, you know, Dubois shouldn't
write that much fiction.

00:57:02.400 --> 00:57:05.350
This is an extraordinary story,
and the crucial scene in it

00:57:05.350 --> 00:57:09.230
is this young Black man has gone to school

00:57:09.230 --> 00:57:12.920
and envisions his sort of
future kind of progress

00:57:12.920 --> 00:57:14.700
in the world and becoming educated

00:57:14.700 --> 00:57:18.210
and becoming sort of, it's a commentary

00:57:18.210 --> 00:57:22.283
on Booker T. Washington
style kind of, you know,

00:57:23.788 --> 00:57:25.669
Black improvement and
then sort of betterment.

00:57:25.669 --> 00:57:27.850
And then there's this catastrophe
where he's at a concert

00:57:27.850 --> 00:57:31.410
and the usher taps him on the shoulder

00:57:31.410 --> 00:57:33.326
and says he has to leave.

00:57:33.326 --> 00:57:35.570
The white woman sitting next
to him has become uncomfortable

00:57:35.570 --> 00:57:37.610
by his presence, and then it turns out

00:57:37.610 --> 00:57:42.610
this woman is with this white
childhood friend of his.

00:57:42.930 --> 00:57:45.690
The whole story sort of
spirals toward disaster.

00:57:45.690 --> 00:57:50.690
But the, as he's listening to the music,

00:57:50.705 --> 00:57:53.603
and it's Wagner's "Lohengrin,"
the prelude to "Lohengrin"

00:57:53.603 --> 00:57:56.190
He has this vision of some other worlds

00:57:56.190 --> 00:57:58.885
that could be achieved that
he could sort of step into,

00:57:58.885 --> 00:58:00.168
and it's denied to him.

00:58:00.168 --> 00:58:03.160
And at the end of the story,
he's about to be lynched,

00:58:03.160 --> 00:58:06.500
but the music is still
running through his head.

00:58:06.500 --> 00:58:07.720
And I don't think Dubois,

00:58:07.720 --> 00:58:09.633
and there's an ironic aspect to this,

00:58:10.646 --> 00:58:11.660
but there's also, I think a sense,

00:58:11.660 --> 00:58:14.443
and when Wagner, when
Dubois talks about Wagner,

00:58:15.406 --> 00:58:17.860
he talks about he's transported by Wagner,

00:58:17.860 --> 00:58:20.360
and it's sort of, there's
a vision here of something

00:58:20.360 --> 00:58:22.800
in which one can believe and one can trust

00:58:22.800 --> 00:58:24.490
that we can sort of carry with us

00:58:24.490 --> 00:58:28.386
and that can sort of
inform our own struggles

00:58:28.386 --> 00:58:31.260
toward a better future.

00:58:31.260 --> 00:58:34.640
And so it's this, it's not sardonic

00:58:36.066 --> 00:58:38.890
or sort of purely ironic
sort of circumstance

00:58:38.890 --> 00:58:39.940
that he's setting up.

00:58:40.884 --> 00:58:42.980
It's just a tragic, it's a
tragic one rather than ironic,

00:58:42.980 --> 00:58:44.760
but it's quite powerful.

00:58:44.760 --> 00:58:46.100
And it's just another,

00:58:46.100 --> 00:58:47.910
just very surprising unexpected way

00:58:47.910 --> 00:58:52.750
in which Wagner plays a role
in the culture of this period,

00:58:52.750 --> 00:58:53.583
the Fantasque.

00:58:55.966 --> 00:58:56.963
- Wow, all right, well, we're out of time.

00:58:57.820 --> 00:59:00.006
We have, you know, 20 more
questions from the audience,

00:59:00.006 --> 00:59:01.005
and I had 20 more.

00:59:01.005 --> 00:59:02.764
So I feel like we could
have gone on for hours,

00:59:02.764 --> 00:59:07.120
but I think we have to leave
it there unfortunately.

00:59:07.120 --> 00:59:10.470
And thank you so much for this.

00:59:10.470 --> 00:59:14.530
It was really, I just thought,
you know, fascinating,

00:59:14.530 --> 00:59:19.424
and I recommend to
everyone to get the book.

00:59:19.424 --> 00:59:22.327
I also want to do a pitch
for our last program

00:59:23.680 --> 00:59:24.970
which is gonna be in two weeks.

00:59:24.970 --> 00:59:27.742
It was gonna be in one week,
but it's actually gonna be

00:59:27.742 --> 00:59:30.964
in two weeks, which is a
discussion with Jake McAuley

00:59:30.964 --> 00:59:33.660
about his new book, "The
House of Fragile Things."

00:59:33.660 --> 00:59:36.261
So mark your calendars for that.

00:59:36.261 --> 00:59:38.563
And again, thank you so much, Alex.

00:59:38.563 --> 00:59:41.421
I really appreciate your coming.

00:59:41.421 --> 00:59:43.040
- A pleasure, thank you so much.

00:59:43.040 --> 00:59:45.080
- And thank you to
everyone in the audience.

00:59:45.080 --> 00:59:45.913
Bye.

